{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And the Avvim who lived in open towns as far as Gaza.</b> He says that the Avvim living in the towns of Chatzerim, which have no wall around them, as far as Gaza — which is the border of the Canaanite, as it is said (Genesis 10:19), \"And the border of the Canaanite was from Sidon, as you come to Gerar, as far as Gaza\" — the Caphtorim who came out of Caphtor destroyed them, and they dwell in their place. Now the Caphtorim are descendants of Egypt (ibid. verses 13–14), and they are not of the gift of Abraham; their land belongs to Israel, because the Caphtorim captured it from the children of Canaan. And when the verse said concerning the land of the Philistines (Genesis 26:3), \"For to you and to your seed I will give all these lands,\" it was for this reason: because that land originally belonged to the children of Canaan. And for this reason Israel dispossessed in the days of Joshua the five lords of the Philistines — the Gazite, the Ashdodite, the Ashkelonite, the Gittite, and the Ekronite — and the Avvim (Joshua 13:3), for they all lived within the border of the Canaanite, in Gaza and in Ashkelon and in Ashdod and Ekron, and they are called by the name of their places. For they destroyed the Avvim, who were of the children of Canaan, who lived within the border of the Canaanite from Sidon, as you come to Gerar, as far as Gaza, and dwelt in their place. And Sidon too belongs to the Philistines, as it is written (Joel 4:4), \"And moreover, what are you to me, O Tyre and Sidon, and all the regions of Philistia?\"\n\nIn my opinion, the Avvim are the Hivvim, for it is the practice in Scripture to interchange the names, as we have said (above, verse 10), when they refer to one matter, and the snake twists itself along the way. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 26:7), Rabbi Elazar ben Rabbi Shimon said that they were skilled in the dust like the snake; in Galilee they cry out לחויא עויא (\"snake\" / \"Avvite\"). And the Sages were accustomed to call it עכנא and to call it חכינא. And they said (Ruth Rabbah 5:6), \"And he entreated him and heard his supplication\" (II Chronicles 33:13) — Rabbi Levi said: in Arabia they cry out לחתירה עתירה (\"to entreat\" / \"supplication\"). And they said (Eichah Rabbah 2:2), \"How does He darken in His anger, HaShem\" — Rabbi Chama bar Rabbi Chanina said: How did HaShem decree in His wrath? There is a place where they cry out לחיבא איבא (\"to decree\" / \"enemy\"); חקים עקים (\"decrees\" / \"bent\"). This is common in the language; and what does עויא say regarding this matter about Rabbi Chiya? And so our Sages were accustomed to say <b>hesach hada'at</b> (\"distraction of the mind\") in place of <b>hese' hada'at</b> (\"removal of the mind\"). And in the Jerusalem Talmud it is always <b>hisio</b>, and in the Mishnah, \"and he removed his heart from eating.\" And so the commentators said concerning עושו ובאו כל הגוים (Joel 4:11) that it is חושו (\"hasten\"). And in the Mekhilta (Bo 7), Rabbi Yoshiya says: do not read ופסחתי but rather ופסעתי (\"and I will step\"), for the Place, blessed be He, leaps over the houses of the children of Israel. He made it from the root פסיעות (\"steps\") with the interchange of ח\"י and עי\"ן.\n\nNow he completed informing us of all the land of the Hivvim that was lost from them: part of it belongs to Esau and is forbidden to us, and part of it belongs to the Caphtorim and we may take it from them. And in Bereshit Rabbah (ibid.) I found that the Avvim are the Rephaim. They said concerning the verse, \"The Nephilim were in the earth\" (Genesis 6:4): the Rephaim are the Avvim; Rephaim, because everyone who sees them, his heart grows weak as in faintness; Avvim, because they were skilled in the dust like the snake, etc. And they further said there (Bereshit Rabbah 44:23), concerning the gift of Abraham: Rabbi Dostai in the name of Rabbi Shmuel — because he does not mention Hivvi here, therefore he brings Rephaim in their place.\n\nWhen I investigated this, I found that the matter is true, established, correct, and enduring. For the seven nations that Israel dispossessed, which Moses our master mentioned to them, as he said (Deuteronomy 7:1), \"And He will dispossess many nations before you — the Hittite and the Girgashite and the Amorite and the Canaanite and the Perizzite and the Hivvite and the Jebusite, seven nations greater and mightier\" — six of these are mentioned in the gift of Abraham, but the Hivvite is not mentioned there. At any rate, he is the Rephaim, or one of Keni, Kenizi, and Kadmoni. And our Sages (Bereshit Rabbah ibid.) all say that Keni, Kenizi, and Kadmoni — Israel did not inherit them, and He is destined to give them as an inheritance, and concerning them it is said, \"And if YHWH your Elohim enlarge your border and give you all the land that He promised to give to your fathers\" (Deuteronomy 19:8). And so they are not mentioned anywhere as having been inherited in the days of Joshua. If so, the Rephaim that He gave to Abraham is the Hivvite, who is the sixth son of Canaan (Genesis 10:17). His father called him Hivvi, but in the days of Abraham they called him Rephaim.\n\nIn my opinion, it is an epithet derived from Hivvi, after the manner of חור פתן ומאורת צפעוני (Isaiah 11:8), for those hidden in the dust the language calls Rephaim, as in הרפאים יחוללו מתחת מים ושכניהם (Job 26:5), and וארץ רפאים תפיל (Isaiah 26:19). Now the children of this Hivvi multiplied greatly, and when the families of the Canaanite spread out, this family took large lands. Some of them were called the land of Rephaim, and some Horim, and some Avvim, and collectively they are all Hivvi, and all Rephaim — just as among our people there are Ephraim, Judah, and Israel, and collectively all of them are Israel. And all these names are descriptive of the Hivvi, for since his name was Hivvi, they called him after חויא (snake) Rephaim, for the snake dwells in holes of dust, and Hori and Avvi are after the name of the snake, as I have explained (above, verse 10). And the Holy One, blessed be He, gave to Abraham the Rephaim in their entirety, and Moses mentions to Israel the Hivvi — the first name that his father called him — for the land of Rephaim in the days of Moses had already been divided, and part of it is forbidden to Israel.\n\nNow the verse comes to mention here the matter of all the land of the Hivvi — which are the Rephaim — and everything derived from it. He said first that the Emim too are of the Rephaim, and Moav dispossessed them. Then he returned and said that the Hori, who are also of the Rephaim, Esav dispossessed. And he returned again and said that the land of the children of Ammon is also the land of Rephaim, and all of this He forbade to Israel. And he further recounted that the Caphtorim too, who came out of Caphtor, took from their land, and it is permitted to Israel. And Joshua mentioned the land of the Perizzite and the Rephaim (Joshua 17:15).\n\nBut Rashi wrote (Rashi on Deuteronomy 2:23): the Avvim are of the Philistines, for they are counted among them in the book of Joshua (13:3) — the Gazite, the Ashdodite, the Ashkelonite, the Gittite, the Ekronite, and the Avvim. And because of the oath that Abraham swore to Avimelech, Israel could not go into their land and drive them out of their land, so I brought the Caphtorim upon them and they destroyed them and dwelt in their place. And now you are permitted to take from their hand. These words do not sit well with me, for the verse does not count the Avvim among the Philistines; rather, it counts the five lords of the Philistines and mentions them, and afterward mentions the Avvim as a different people. It says that from the Shihor which is before Egypt as far as the border of Ekron northward, all is counted as the land of Canaan — the five lords of the Philistines which it enumerates, and also the Avvim, who are a different nation. And according to the Rav's words, behold the Avvim whom the Caphtorim dispossessed were permitted — but the rest of the lords of the Philistines, who permitted them, and why should Israel conquer all of them, since it is Joshua who conquered the Philistines and the Avvim? And furthermore, why should the land of the Philistines belong to Abraham at all, and why did Avimelech need his covenant in this matter? The Philistines were descendants of Egypt, and so were the Caphtorim, and they are not of the ten nations. Rather, the matter is the reverse: the Caphtorim and the Philistines destroyed [some] of the Canaanites, for Avimelech was king of Gerar, and the Gazite lived in Gaza, and Sidon is a Philistine city — and these are within the border of the Canaanite, as it is said, \"And the border of the Canaanite was from Sidon, as you come to Gerar, as far as Gaza.\"\n\nAnd what seems right in my eyes is that the Philistines and the Caphtorim are reckoned as one people, as the verse also says (Jeremiah 47:4), \"For YHWH despoils the Philistines, the remnant of the isle of Caphtor.\" And this is the meaning of <b>the Caphtorim who came out of Caphtor</b>: a small group of people went out from Caphtor to sojourn wherever they could find a place, and they destroyed the Avvim who lived as far as Gaza — who were of the seed of Canaan — and dwelt in their place, in Gaza and Ashkelon and Ashdod and Gath and Ekron, all within the borders of Canaan. And they divided it among their five lords, and the name of those districts is Pelesheth, and they too were called Philistines. And there remained a remnant of the Avvim's land, as Joshua mentioned, and this is what he said (ibid.): Philistines, the remnant of the isle of Caphtor — for they are Philistines. And this is the meaning of \"from there the Philistines and the Caphtorim went out\" (Genesis 10:14), as I explained there.\n\nAnd what the verse says (Amos 9:7), \"Have not I brought up Israel out of the land of Egypt, and the Philistines from Caphtor, and Aram from Kir?\" — it recounts the wars of YHWH, for He brought up Israel from the land of Egypt with signs and wonders and with war, and He brought up the Philistines from Caphtor their land, for He brought them out from there to give them the Avvim in a great miracle, and they destroyed them and dwelt in their place, and they left Caphtor for their brothers. Or the matter may be as I have already mentioned: the Avvim, the children of Canaan who originally lived in the land — their name was Philistines, for the name of the land is Pelesheth, and the Caphtorim who came out of Caphtor, when they destroyed them, exiled some of them to Caphtor, their land, which is part of the land of Egypt, near their brothers, and YHWH redeemed them from there and they returned to their place — we do not know in which generation among the generations. And behold, the Philistines are inhabitants of the land of Canaan, and therefore the verse says (Zephaniah 2:5), \"The word of YHWH is upon you, O Canaan, land of the Philistines; and I will destroy you, that there be no inhabitant.\" And so Israel did not dispossess the Philistines until the time of the oath had passed — until three generations of the Philistines had died in that land of Avimelech's. And so they said in the Midrash (Mekhilta, beginning of Beshalach): \"for it is near\" — the oath that Abraham swore to Avimelech is near; his grandson still lives.\n\nThis is what is fitting and correct regarding this matter. But in the Gemara in the chapter Ve'elu Trefot (Chulin 60a), they stated a dispute according to Rashi's words, and Rav said there: the Avvim came from Teiman, and a Tanna taught like him, and Teiman is the seed of Edom, as it is written (Jeremiah 49:7), \"Concerning Edom, thus says YHWH of Hosts: Is there no more wisdom in Teiman?\" And this is possible. The verse may be saying that the Avvim, who are of the children of Esav — part of their land was purified through the hand of the Caphtorim, just as Ammon and Moav were purified through Sichon. And what I have written is what is good and right."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The word טהרה (\"was purified\") appears to be a technical term Ramban borrows from the rabbinic discussion in Chulin 60a, where land conquered by a third party becomes \"purified\" (i.e., freed from the original prohibition) for Israel to take. The singular feminine form agrees with ארצם (her land), reading the collective singular."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:46.843478972Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}